<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Thuringia</h1><p class="st-meta">631 CE – 719 CE (approx.) · capital Würzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Frankish frontier duchy in central Germany, set up against the Slavs, whose dukes briefly ruled almost independently before Carolingian absorption.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-thuringians">Kingdom of the Thuringians</a> in 531, the area was ruled by Frankish officials. Around 633 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">King Dagobert I</a> appointed Radulf duke to defend the eastern frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a>, then raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Radulf later defeated Dagobert's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-iii">Sigebert III</a> in 641 and ruled as a near-independent king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> was held by the Hedenid dukes, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> on the Main. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heden-ii">Heden II</a> supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibrord">Willibrord</a>, who received estates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> in 704, and a church was founded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienberg-fortress">Marienberg</a> at Würzburg. The duchy guarded the border with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heden-ii">Heden II</a> disappears around 717 to 719, perhaps after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vincy">Battle of Vincy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> under direct Frankish control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> reorganised its church, founding the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 742. The region later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-thuringia">Landgraviate of Thuringia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 631 CE – 719 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:72.26%;width:1.75%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>631 CE – 719 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Würzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Radulf, Heden II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old High German, Thuringian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Frankish Thuringia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=675&amp;focus=duchy-of-thuringia" aria-label="Duchy of Thuringia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4908 444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Thuringia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5228 700L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5228 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Würzburg. <a href="/atlas?year=675&amp;focus=duchy-of-thuringia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
